{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ANt4EpLZWLSJNx6ppCmbZx","type":"tool_result","content":"48:Pairwise-comparison leaderboards have become a central instrument of model evaluation. Chatbot Arena aggregates crowdsourced votes between two anonymous chatbots~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA}. MT-Bench collects expert and GPT-4 judgments on a fixed question set~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW}. Sports ratings descend from the same paired-comparison tradition~\\citep{Elo1978TheRO}. In each case a Bradley--Terry (BT) model~\\citep{Bradley1952RankAO} turns the comparisons into scores, and the scores into a ranking. Rankings are then used to choose models, to advertise releases, and to compare research systems. Their confidence intervals usually come from resampling or asymptotic arguments~\\citep{Efron1979BootstrapMA,Gao2021UncertaintyQI}. These describe \\emph{random} variation, not how much \\emph{specific} data the conclusion rests on. Recent work shows that crowdsourced leaderboards can be moved deliberately~\\citep{Min2025ImprovingYM}, that evaluation practice shapes them~\\citep{Singh2025TheLI}, and that dropping a small number of preferences can alter top Arena rankings~\\citep{Huang2025DroppingJA}. What remains open is how this sensitivity \\emph{compares across evaluation designs}, and how the answer depends on what is measured.\n129:Table~\\ref{tab:cases} lists the cases. The four designs are crowdsourced votes from the Chatbot Arena 140k human-preference release~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA}; expert human and GPT-4 pairwise judgments of MT-Bench answers~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW}; and NBA games from two seasons of the FiveThirtyEight Elo record, the 2014 and 2015 seasons in that file's labeling, with each game a comparison between two teams, following the long use of BT models for sports tournaments~\\citep{Cattelan2013DynamicBM}. We fit a static BT model to each season, without the time-varying strengths such models can include. Under the harness's stated protocol, Arena ``tie'' and ``both bad'' votes and MT-Bench tie judgments become ties ($y=\\tfrac12$); NBA games have no ties. Each design contributes three cases: drop/top-1, flip/top-1, and drop/top-$k$. With the two controls this gives 17 cases. Method choices were made on separate development data, stated by the harness to be the Arena 55k release and the NBA 1983 season. The harness is also stated to exclude final rows whose content hash appears in the development data. Neither the split nor its overlap can be inspected in our artifacts. The 17 cases were then run once in a sealed evaluation under seeds 101, 102, and 103. Every episode either returns a set that the harness verifies, or counts as a failure; none failed. The evaluation completed and passed all of its validity gates.\n358:The study pre-declared one inferential claim: the evaluated search would find strictly smaller verified sets than the one-step influence ordering. Specifically, the bootstrap lower bound of the mean clipped $\\log_2(k_{\\text{infl}}/\\kver)$ would have to exceed 0. The selected configuration is the plain influence ordering with verified prefix search, so this ratio is 0.0 with CI [0.0, 0.0], and the claim is not supported. The evaluation still completed and qualified on every validity gate. The sizes in this paper are therefore valid measurements, and the negative outcome concerns only algorithmic improvement.\n379:Large language models were used extensively and are disclosed here. An autonomous LLM-based research agent proposed the study design within the task's constraints. The evaluated perturbation finder was not written by the agent: it is the provided starter (baseline) configuration, seeded before the search. The agent designed three refined search configurations, but their source is not among our artifacts and none was scored. The agent wrote the analysis and plotting scripts and drafted this manuscript, including its framing and text. Every number in the paper comes from the sealed evaluation record produced by executing the code, and the derivation scripts are included. Bibliographic metadata was retrieved from the Semantic Scholar API. Huang et al.~(2025) is cited by title only, as part of the provided background reading list. No result or method in this paper is taken from it. No paid API or GPU was used for the experiments.\n143:      \"claim\": \"The sealed evaluation completed, qualified, and passed all validity gates.\",","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"682781d2-f2de-4026-ae3f-0543bfb876d5","uuid":"3c492abc-4347-4608-825d-ccff7becdf90","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:46:02.433Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"48:Pairwise-comparison leaderboards have become a central instrument of model evaluation. Chatbot Arena aggregates crowdsourced votes between two anonymous chatbots~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA}. MT-Bench collects expert and GPT-4 judgments on a fixed question set~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW}. Sports ratings descend from the same paired-comparison tradition~\\citep{Elo1978TheRO}. In each case a Bradley--Terry (BT) model~\\citep{Bradley1952RankAO} turns the comparisons into scores, and the scores into a ranking. Rankings are then used to choose models, to advertise releases, and to compare research systems. Their confidence intervals usually come from resampling or asymptotic arguments~\\citep{Efron1979BootstrapMA,Gao2021UncertaintyQI}. These describe \\emph{random} variation, not how much \\emph{specific} data the conclusion rests on. Recent work shows that crowdsourced leaderboards can be moved deliberately~\\citep{Min2025ImprovingYM}, that evaluation practice shapes them~\\citep{Singh2025TheLI}, and that dropping a small number of preferences can alter top Arena rankings~\\citep{Huang2025DroppingJA}. What remains open is how this sensitivity \\emph{compares across evaluation designs}, and how the answer depends on what is measured.\n129:Table~\\ref{tab:cases} lists the cases