built on a refit-verified, influence-ordered perturbation finder; the finder is the provided baseline (starter) configuration, not a new algorithm of ours. The protocol covers two perturbation types (dropping and flipping comparisons) and two targets (top-1 and top-$k$ set). It is checked against a planted case with an enumerated minimum, a row-permutation control, and seed replay (\\S\\ref{sec:method}, \\S\\ref{sec:res-validity}).\n65:\\caption{Protocol overview. Comparison rows from four evaluation designs are fit with a ridge Bradley--Terry model. Rows are ordered by their one-step influence on the decisive margin: leader vs.\\ runner-up for top-1, or the $k$-th vs.\\ $(k{+}1)$-th player for a top-$k$ set. The shortest prefix whose removal or outcome reversal changes the target is found by exponential and binary search. Every probe is an exact refit, and the final set is re-verified independently. Sizes are reported as a count $\\kver$ and as a fraction $\\kver/n$. A random-subset comparator, a direct leader--challenger heuristic, a planted-minimum control, a row-permutation control, and seed replay calibrate the instrument.}\n126:These are also part of the harness's stated protocol. \\emph{Planted minimum:} a synthetic three-player case with a near-tied leader pair. The harness computes its exact minimal drop size by brute-force enumeration over per-pair winner counts, which are sufficient statistics for \\eqref{eq:bt}. \\emph{Row permutation:} the Arena drop/top-1 case with its rows presented in a different order. A correct instrument must return the same size. \\emph{Seed replay:} each case runs under three seeds, and one episode is replayed and its output hash compared. Before any search, the harness is stated to check each case. It rejects cases with a degenerate decisive margin ($m\\le 10\\tau$). It also requires that the empty perturbation leaves the target unchanged and that a refit on reversed row order reproduces $\\hat\\theta$.\n161:Table~\\ref{tab:validity} collects the evidence that the measured sizes mean what we claim. On the planted synthetic case, the verified drop size is 8 and the control score is 0. The harness defines this score as $\\min(0,\\log_2(k^\\star/\\kver))$ with $k^\\star$ its enumerated minimum. Under that stated definition, a value of 0 means the instrument returned the brute-force minimum, so here, at least, the upper bound is tight. Under row permutation, the Arena drop/top-1 size is 62 in both presentations (control score 0), so the result does not depend on row order. All 15 real-case sizes are identical across seeds 101, 102, and 103. The replayed episode reproduced its output hash (replay match 1.0), and no episode failed. Since the finder is deterministic, seed agreement shows reproducibility. It does \\emph{not} measure sampling uncertainty (\\S\\ref{sec:limitations}).\n164:% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.replay_match]]\n191:Replay of one episode & match $=1.0$ & identical output hash \\\\\n358:The study pre-declared one inferential claim: the evaluated search would find strictly smaller verified sets than the one-step influence ordering. Specifically, the bootstrap lower bound of the mean clipped $\\log_2(k_{\\text{infl}}/\\kver)$ would have to exceed 0. The selected configuration is the plain influence ordering with verified prefix search, so this ratio is 0.0 with CI [0.0, 0.0], and the claim is not supported. The evaluation still completed and qualified on every validity gate. The sizes in this paper are therefore valid measurements, and the negative outcome concerns only algorithmic improvement.\n371:Python, CPU only; the finder depends only on NumPy and the standard library. The full sealed evaluation runs the 17 cases under three seeds plus one replay. Recorded episode time was about 16\\,s per seed; the record does not specify which harness steps (e.g., comparator runs or verification refits) this duration includes.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_